<div class="blurb">
<div class="header"><h2>the problem</h2></div>
<div class="body">

<p>The rest of this site works through the chemistry, the labeling rules, and the health concerns piece by piece. The summary version of <em>the problem</em> is that MSG is everywhere, its presence is mostly invisible on the label, and the consequences land on the consumer's body rather than on the manufacturer's disclosure.</p>

<p>MSG is a <span class="highlight">workhorse ingredient of the processed-food and quick-service restaurant industries</span> <sup><a href="#src-qsr-1">qsr-1</a>, <a href="#src-qsr-2">qsr-2</a>, <a href="#src-qsr-3">qsr-3</a>, <a href="#src-qsr-4">qsr-4</a></sup>. The labeling rule that makes "monosodium glutamate" appear on a panel is the one that requires the word only when MSG has been added as an ingredient &mdash; it does not catch free-glutamate carriers, finished dishes that form MSG in situ during cooking, or proprietary seasoning blends (see <a href="#section-100-what-is-msg-dsg">what is msg/dsg</a> and <a href="#section-140-yeast-extract-and-sodium-can-form-msg-during-cooking">yeast extract and sodium can form msg during cooking</a> for the full labeling gap, and <a href="#section-155-msg-in-us-military-rations">msg in us military rations</a> for the MRE case where the civilian rule does not apply at all). Restaurants are not required to disclose use of MSG as a trade secret, and the label on a packaged product never has to state the quantity even when "monosodium glutamate" does appear on the panel. "No MSG" on a menu or a package, strictly read, is impossible &mdash; only "no <em>added</em> MSG" is an accurate statement, and even that allows the in-situ loophole. The same opacity carries over to the QSR websites themselves: allergen and ingredient pages often bury MSG inside "spice," "seasoning," or "natural flavor" lists, omit it from marketing-facing nutrition pages entirely, or require a click through a half-dozen PDFs to find any ingredient disclosure at all &mdash; so even a consumer motivated to check ahead of time is funnelled past the answer.</p>

<p>Even the major snack-food brands acknowledge the practice in their own words. Frito-Lay has stated: <em>"In order to maintain the taste that consumers have grown to love... we use extremely low levels of MSG in some products to enhance flavor"</em> <sup><a href="#src-frito">frito</a>, <a href="#src-frito-2">frito-2</a>, <a href="#src-frito-3">frito-3</a>, <a href="#src-frito-4">frito-4</a></sup>. The MSG + DSG synergy that amplifies both the sodium and the glutamate load of a finished dish, and the cumulative and excitotoxicity-based health effects that follow from regular exposure, are detailed in <a href="#section-200-health-concerns">health concerns</a>; this section is only the framing.</p>

<p>The result is a population that absorbs meaningful daily MSG &mdash; most of it undeclared and untracked &mdash; and treats the downstream effects with over-the-counter remedies: water pills for the bloating, pain relievers for the headaches, antacids for the reflux. That is, the same symptoms catalogued in <a href="#section-200-health-concerns">health concerns</a> are routinely <span class="highlight">managed with pharmaceuticals</span> rather than addressed by removing the dietary cause. The pharmaceutical layer is its own burden on the body; it is also, conveniently for the food industry, invisible on the package. Avoiding the cause would mean knowing it is there in the first place, and the labeling rules are not built to tell you.</p>

</div>
